  <main id="doc" class="pb-16">
    <nav class="components" aria-label="Article contents">
      <svg class="components-glyph" viewBox="0 0 30.056 30.026" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#node-icon" /></svg>
      <p class="label eyebrow">THE NODE · CONTENTS</p>
      {chapters.map((c, ci) => (
        <>
          <p class="label chapter">{ci + 1} · {c.title}</p>
          {c.sections.map((s) => (
            <a href={`#${s.id}`}><b>{byId[s.id].n}</b>{s.title}</a>
          ))}
        </>
      ))}
    </nav>

    <div class="doc-body">
      <article class="prose-page" id="node">
        <p class="lead">
          Radio, sensors, compute, storage, and the network paths into them can all be owned
          outright and run on local power. Infrastructure built that way keeps working when a
          carrier, a cloud provider, or the electrical grid does not, and the capacity it is not
          using can be given to somebody else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Of the layers a homestead needs, information is the hardest to get right. Food, water,
          and energy have mature, well-documented solutions. Secure, modern, resilient
          infrastructure for carrying, storing, and reasoning over information does not.
        </p>

        <div class="grid layers" role="group" aria-label="The four layers of a homestead">
          <div class="panel layer">
            <h3>Food</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><Icon name="fish" />Blue tilapia stock</li>
              <li><Icon name="sprout" />Plants fed on fish waste</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="panel layer">
            <h3>Water</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><Icon name="droplet" />Well to the aquifer</li>
              <li><Icon name="cloud-rain" />Rainwater collection</li>
              <li><Icon name="cloud-drizzle" />Atmospheric water generation</li>
              <li><Icon name="filter" />Filtration and purification</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="panel layer">
            <h3>Energy</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><Icon name="sun" />Solar, wind, hydro</li>
              <li><Icon name="flame" />Biogas and biodiesel</li>
              <li><Icon name="battery-charging" />Battery storage</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="panel layer">
            <h3>Information</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><Icon name="radio-tower" />Radio, satellite, broadcast</li>
              <li><Icon name="cctv" />Sensing and cameras</li>
              <li><Icon name="cpu" />Compute, models, storage</li>
              <li><Icon name="shield" />Private paths in and out</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          One principle runs through every technology here. Whoever owns a machine decides what
          runs on it and who keeps access when the terms change. Renting the machine hands that
          decision to somebody else.
        </p>

        <div class="node-arts">
          <img
            class="node-art"
            src="/images/MyNode.png"
            alt="Line drawing of the node: a trailer-mounted mast carrying mesh antennas, a GNSS dome, a weather sensor array, and a directional grid antenna, above solar panels and the equipment box"
          />
          <img
            class="node-art"
            src="/images/MyCompute.png"
            alt="Line drawing of the compute rack: a full-height cabinet holding stacked vented server chassis, a panel of network ports, and rows of hot-swap power supply modules above its feet"
          />
        </div>
      </article>

      <section class="marks" id="mesh-radio">
        <Fragment set:html={head('mesh-radio')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Mesh radio carries text messages between inexpensive radios with no SIM card, no
          account, and no monthly bill. Every radio added to a mesh extends the range of every
          radio already on it.
        </p>
        <!-- the same visual language as the field diagram above: mono tags, dashed
             range rings, the map's mesh/node glyphs reused via <use> (same DOM).
             two layers per transmission, the idiom mesh simulators use: an expanding
             wave (the broadcast, RF has no direction) plus a packet dot riding the
             front along the route (the message's logical path). the traffic script
             below runs back-to-back exchanges (contacts to contacts, strangers to
             strangers, either relaying for the other), map-style: pooled one-shot
             pulses, rx flashes, wave arcs keyed only while transmitting. a node
             pulses only when it transmits or rebroadcasts, never on receive alone.
             static-legible under reduced motion (the script never starts). -->
        <figure class="diagram meshdiagram">
          <div class="scroller">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 1000 560"
              role="img"
              aria-label="Diagram of the local mesh over contour terrain: my node plus my contacts' solar nodes, marked with sun badges, among other community radios. Long-dashed accent lines link my network on the private shared-key channel; dotted grey lines link every other pair of radios in range on the public default channel. A dashed ring shows my node's radio range: contacts beyond it reach me through nodes between us. Animated packets flow continuously, contacts messaging contacts and strangers messaging strangers, any radio relaying either; a key badge marks the sender and receiver of the exchange in flight, the two nodes able to read it. Each hop is an expanding radio wave, and a node shows its wave marks and pulses only while transmitting or relaying."
            >
              <!-- real SRTM contours for a random inland frame (gen-mesh-terrain.mjs);
                   deliberately not the field map's terrain -->
              <g class="contours">
                {meshContours.map((c) => <path d={c.d} class:list={[c.level % meshIndexStep === 0 && 'index']} />)}
              </g>

              <!-- my node's RF range: the contacts outside this ring reach me only
                   through the nodes between us -->
              <circle class="range mine" cx="760" cy="295" r="290" />

              <!-- the standing topology: every in-range pair linked, private on top -->
              {meshLinks.map((l) => (
                <line class={l.priv ? 'priv-link' : 'pub-link'} x1={l.x1} y1={l.y1} x2={l.x2} y2={l.y2} />
              ))}

              <!-- transmission pulse pool: the script parks one at whichever node is
                   keying up; it expands to that radio's RF range and dies there -->
              {Array.from({ length: 3 }).map(() => <circle class="pulse" cx="0" cy="0" r="290" />)}

              <!-- the packet in transit, driven along each route by the script -->
              <circle class="packet" cx="0" cy="0" r="4.5" />

              {meshNodes.map((n, i) => (
                <g class:list={['mn', n.contact && 'contact', n.mine && 'm-mine']}>
                  {n.mine ? (
                    <svg data-mesh={i} x={n.x - 26} y={n.y - 26} width="52" height="52" viewBox="0 0 30.056 30.026" class="glyph"><use href="#node-icon" /></svg>
                  ) : (
                    <svg data-mesh={i} x={n.x - 12} y={n.y - 12} width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="glyph"><use href="#mesh-icon" /></svg>
                  )}
                  {n.contact && (
                    <svg x={n.x + 14} y={n.y - 28} width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="sun"><use href="#sun-icon" /></svg>
                  )}
                </g>
              ))}

              <!-- key badges: the script parks these on the sender and receiver
                   for the duration of each exchange -->
              <svg class="key" x="0" y="0" width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#key-icon" /></svg>
              <svg class="key" x="0" y="0" width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#key-icon" /></svg>

              <g class="legend">
                <svg x="40" y="494" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="sun"><use href="#sun-icon" /></svg>
                <text class="tag sub" x="60" y="505">SOLAR CONTACT NODE</text>
                <svg x="40" y="519" width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="key" style="opacity: 1"><use href="#key-icon" /></svg>
                <text class="tag sub" x="60" y="530">SENDER + RECEIVER KEY</text>
                <line class="priv-link" x1="250" y1="500" x2="274" y2="500" />
                <text class="tag sub" x="292" y="505">PRIVATE CHANNEL · SHARED KEY</text>
                <line class="pub-link" x1="250" y1="525" x2="274" y2="525" />
                <text class="tag sub" x="292" y="530">PUBLIC CHANNEL · OPEN TO ANYONE</text>
              </g>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Spectrum is the range of radio frequencies a transmitter may use. Some of it is set
          aside for anyone to use without a license, and mesh radios work there, at power levels
          low enough that a small solar panel and a battery keep one running. Open-source
          firmware does the rest.
        </p>
        <p>
          Range comes from rebroadcast. Each radio repeats every message it has not heard before,
          up to a set number of hops, so two radios too far apart to hear each other are carried
          by the radios in between. No radio holds a map of the network. A radio that can hear
          both sides is enough.
        </p>
        <p>
          Messages travel encrypted. A private group shares one key for its own channel, and a
          message addressed to one person is encrypted with that person's own key, so a radio
          passing it along forwards something it cannot read. The same radio also carries the
          public channel and relays for strangers on it.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          This is the link that survives everything else. Where the radios in a chain run on
          their own solar power, the messages keep moving after the electrical grid, the cell
          network, and the internet have all stopped.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="amateur-relay">
        <Fragment set:html={head('amateur-relay')} />
        <p class="lead">
          An amateur radio repeater turns a handheld radio that reaches a few blocks into one
          that reaches a whole city. Operating on the amateur bands requires a license, and the
          license carries obligations set by regulation.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          A repeater sits on high ground or a tall mast. It listens on one frequency and
          retransmits what it hears on another at the same instant. Two handhelds talking
          directly reach a few miles in the open and much less between buildings. Through a
          repeater, a handheld reaches every radio the mast reaches, including while its operator
          is moving.
        </p>
        <p>
          The amateur bands allow far more power than unlicensed spectrum, plus voice and a wide
          range of data modes. Three rules come with that. Stations identify themselves on the
          air, transmissions stay unencrypted, and the service is not for commercial traffic.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Most repeaters are open to any licensed operator in range, who gets exactly the same
          coverage as the machine's owner. That coverage is already standing when the grid and
          the carrier networks are not, and it reaches far past anything a handheld manages
          alone.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="satellite-downlink">
        <Fragment set:html={head('satellite-downlink')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Weather satellites broadcast imagery and warnings continuously to anyone with an
          antenna. Listening is passive, so no company, agency, or provider can close the path.
        </p>
        <!-- the mesh diagram's vocabulary and the map's glyph defs at a completely
             different scale: 30 degrees of latitude instead of a quarter of one, because
             this drawing is about pass geometry and a 2400 km reception horizon
             has to fit on the page for that to be honest. the ring is drawn once
             and means two things at once, which is the mechanism: how far the node
             can hear, and how far each satellite's broadcast reaches. so the test
             for "did this sweep land" is just whether the satellite was inside the
             ring when it keyed, no special casing.
             the terrain is the substance of it. contours and a faint ground wash
             start fully masked and are painted in under each swath as it is swept,
             so the map is built up pass by pass rather than handed over complete.
             the four tracks are parallel and spaced to tile the frame exactly: no
             overlap, no bare ground, because two passes covering the same ground
             would be drawing a coverage claim that is not true. each strip then
             ages continuously from the moment it is finished until its own orbit
             returns to redraw it, so the four are always at four different ages
             and the ground reads as something that has to keep being re-acquired.
             note the deliberate split: the swath paints wherever the imager swept,
             while the node's receive flash stays gated on the ring. the westernmost
             track never comes into range, so it lays down terrain without the node
             ever flashing. that gap between what flew over and what actually landed
             is the point of the section.
             the graticule is the counterweight to all that motion: a 5 degree grid
             outside the mask, so the coordinate frame holds still while the ground
             inside it is acquired, ages and is acquired again. it is unlabeled and
             lands on no round numbers, because the frame's coordinates are
             withheld from the terrain data and the drawing does not get to imply
             what the generator refuses to state.
             no key and no picture inset: every mark here is either decodable from
             its own motion or already established by the field map above, and the
             prose alongside carries the rest.
             receive-only is drawn by omission: --sig is never set on this surface,
             so the node's antenna arcs, the page's established transmit mark,
             physically cannot appear. no pulses from the node, no packet, no keys.
             static-legible under reduced motion (the script never starts): all
             four satellites sit parked along their tracks at different points, each
             with the ground it has swept so far behind it, and one carrier is
             frozen mid-expansion. -->
        <figure class="diagram satdiagram">
          <div class="scroller">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 1000 560"
              role="img"
              aria-label="Diagram of the node's satellite downlinks over a continental frame. A dashed ring around my node marks the reception horizon, about 2,400 kilometers of ground distance, which is also the radius of the ground footprint each passing satellite broadcasts into. Four parallel near-polar ground tracks cross the frame, one orbit walked west each time around, spaced so their imaging swaths tile the frame exactly with no overlap and no gap. A fixed, unlabeled grid of latitude and longitude lines covers the frame and never changes, so the coordinates stay put while the ground inside them comes and goes. The map starts with no terrain at all. Satellites take turns gliding along their tracks, broadcasting continuously as grey expanding rings, and each one paints in a strip of pale ground and elevation contours behind it as it sweeps. A finished strip then fades slowly and evenly for the whole interval until its own orbit comes back to lay it down again, so the four strips sit at four different ages and the ground is never simply there. What the node actually receives is a stricter thing, gated by the ring: while a satellite is inside it the node darkens on each arriving broadcast front. One pass crosses almost overhead, two are middling, and the westernmost stays out of range for its entire transit, so it paints its strip of ground without the node ever flashing. The node never carries a transmit mark, because it only listens."
            >
              <!-- the swath each imager is laying down: one rect per track, grown
                   by the script as its satellite advances. white reveals, so the
                   terrain below exists only where a pass has swept it. a strip is
                   marked .stale one pass before its own orbit returns, which fades
                   it out in time to be repainted. -->
              <mask id="sat-scan-mask" maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x="0" y="0" width="1000" height="560">
                <rect x="0" y="0" width="1000" height="560" fill="black" />
                {satGeom.map((g, i) => (
                  <rect
                    data-swath={i}
                    transform={`translate(${satTracks[i].x1} ${satTracks[i].y1}) rotate(${g.angleDeg})`}
                    x="0"
                    y={-satSwathW / 2}
                    width={g.staticW}
                    height={satSwathW}
                    fill="white"
                  />
                ))}
              </mask>

              <!-- real SRTM contours for a random continental frame
                   (gen-sat-terrain.mjs); its own location, not the other two.
                   the wash rides inside the same masked group, so the tint and the
                   contours appear and age together off one mask. -->
              <g mask="url(#sat-scan-mask)">
                <rect class="scan-wash" x="0" y="0" width="1000" height="560" />
                <g class="contours">
                  {satContours.map((c) => <path d={c.d} class:list={[c.level % satIndexStep === 0 && 'index']} />)}
                </g>
              </g>

              <!-- the graticule sits outside the mask on purpose. it is the frame
                   you read the drawing in, not something a pass produced, so it
                   stays put while the ground under it comes and goes: whatever the
                   satellites have or have not brought down, the coordinates hold
                   still. drawn over the terrain, under everything else. -->
              <g class="graticule">
                {satGratX.map((x) => <line x1={x} y1="0" x2={x} y2="560" />)}
                {satGratY.map((y) => <line x1="0" y1={y} x2="1000" y2={y} />)}
              </g>

              <!-- the reception horizon. clips top and bottom of the frame on
                   purpose: at this scale the real thing is bigger than the view -->
              <circle class="range" cx={satNode.x} cy={satNode.y} r={satRingR} />

              <!-- ground tracks stay put whether or not a satellite is on them,
                   the way a pass predictor draws them -->
              {satTracks.map((t) => <line class="track" x1={t.x1} y1={t.y1} x2={t.x2} y2={t.y2} />)}

              <!-- carrier pool: the script parks one at the satellite's current
                   position and fires it out to the footprint radius -->
              {Array.from({ length: 4 }).map(() => <circle class="carrier" cx="0" cy="0" r={satRingR} />)}
              <!-- one carrier frozen partway to the node, so the static frame still
                   shows a broadcast in flight. the script hides it on start. -->
              <circle class="carrier-static" cx={satParks[0].x} cy={satParks[0].y} r="100" />

              <svg
                data-sat="node"
                x={satNode.x - 26}
                y={satNode.y - 26}
                width="52"
                height="52"
                viewBox="0 0 30.056 30.026"
                class="glyph node-glyph"><use href="#node-icon" /></svg>

              {satParks.map((p, i) => (
                <svg
                  data-sat={i}
                  x={p.x - 13}
                  y={p.y - 13}
                  width="26"
                  height="26"
                  viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                  class="glyph sat-glyph"><use href="#sat-icon" /></svg>
              ))}

            </svg>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Two kinds of satellite are worth hearing. Polar orbiters circle the planet from pole to
          pole while it turns underneath them, so each one eventually passes over everywhere. A
          receiver on the ground decodes each pass into a strip of imagery. Geostationary
          satellites hold one spot above the equator and broadcast the same view continuously,
          carrying imagery alongside forecasts and warnings written as text.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three kinds of image arrive. Visible light shows cloud the way an eye would see it.
          Infrared measures temperature and works at night. Water vapor shows moisture in the air
          that has not yet become weather.
        </p>
        <p>
          Imagery is unreadable without a map underneath it. Map data kept on local disk instead
          of fetched from a service puts reference material under the same ownership as
          everything else here. Ground readings from a weather station complete it: the imagery
          shows what was coming, the sensors record what arrived.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          A receiver asks for nothing. It has no account to suspend and sends no request that
          could be refused, so weather and emergency information keep arriving when every
          terrestrial network in the area is down.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="ota-tv">
        <Fragment set:html={head('ota-tv')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Broadcast television is free and unmetered, and an antenna with a tuner receives news
          and emergency alerts with no internet connection and no account anywhere.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          A transmitter radiates one signal to every receiver in range at once. Nothing counts
          viewers and nothing records who watched, because no receiver ever sends anything back.
          A tuner on the local network turns those channels into streams the whole household can
          watch, and an open-source recorder saves programs to the storage pool on a schedule
          read from the broadcast guide.
        </p>
        <h3>Two standards on the air</h3>
        <p>
          The older United States standard broadcasts in the clear, and any tuner built for it
          decodes the signal. The newer one, ATSC 3.0, adds higher-resolution video, emergency
          alerting that can target a specific area, an internet return channel for stations that
          want one, and datacasting, which carries ordinary files over the broadcast instead of
          video.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some ATSC 3.0 stations encrypt what they send. Decoding those requires a receiver
          carrying a licensed decryption path, which not every device has. A signal broadcast in
          the clear is decodable by any receiver built for the standard, for as long as the
          transmitter keeps running.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          It sits beside the satellite downlinks for the same reason. Both are paths where
          information arrives without being requested, and both keep working when the connection
          a household normally depends on does not.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="beacons">
        <Fragment set:html={head('beacons')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Aircraft and ships broadcast their identity and position continuously, unencrypted, as
          a condition of operating. A small receiver logs every one of them in range, with no
          service, subscription, or license involved.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Aircraft transmit position, altitude, speed, and an identifier on one frequency
          reserved for the purpose. Ships transmit position and course on two marine channels,
          along with a static report giving the vessel's type and length. Neither signal is
          encrypted, because both exist so that other traffic and shore stations can see who is
          nearby.
        </p>
        <p>
          One inexpensive software-defined receiver, the same class of hardware that hears the
          weather satellites, decodes either. Open-source decoders turn the raw signal into a
          live map. An aircraft's make and model is not in the broadcast at all: it comes from
          matching the transmitted identifier against a registry held on local disk.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          It gives a live picture of the traffic around a site with nothing in the path that can
          be metered or withdrawn. A log of it is useful to publish, and publishing types while
          withholding identifiers keeps it that way without following any particular aircraft or
          vessel.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="gnss">
        <Fragment set:html={head('gnss')} />
        <p class="lead">
          A satellite navigation receiver supplies exact time and a fixed position from space, so
          nothing on site has to ask the internet what time it is.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Navigation satellites carry atomic clocks and broadcast the time continuously. A
          receiver on the ground hears several at once and works out both where it is and what
          time it is. Alongside the data, the receiver emits one electrical pulse at the start of
          every second. Timekeeping software uses the message to learn which second it is and the
          pulse to mark exactly when that second begins, which holds the local clock to within
          microseconds of the satellites.
        </p>
        <p>
          That clock then serves every other machine on the local network, so the whole site
          agrees on the time without a single packet leaving it. Several separate satellite
          systems are in orbit, and a receiver that listens to more than one keeps working when
          any single system is unavailable.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Time is a dependency almost nothing declares. Encrypted connections check certificate
          validity against the clock, log entries and sensor readings are only comparable if
          their timestamps agree, and predicting when a satellite will next be overhead is
          arithmetic on the current time. A site with its own clock keeps all of that during an
          outage. The position fix is the second half: it is what aims an antenna and what turns
          a pass prediction into a time to be listening.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="weather-station">
        <Fragment set:html={head('weather-station')} />
        <p class="lead">
          A weather station measures the conditions at one exact place and reports them to open
          data commons instead of to a manufacturer's app.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Sensors read temperature, humidity, air pressure, and wind. Each reading is a few bytes
          sent every several minutes, which is exactly what long-range low-power radio is built
          to carry, so the mesh or a community-run sensor network moves the traffic without any
          wired connection to the instruments.
        </p>
        <p>
          The receiving platforms are commons: any station may feed them and anyone may query
          them. Nobody can reprice a commons, put it behind a login, or shut it down when a
          product line is discontinued, and the readings are useful to people other than whoever
          installed the sensors.
        </p>
        <h3>Listening as measurement</h3>
        <p>
          An all-weather microphone and a classifier running on local hardware identify bird
          species by their calls, which turns a fixed location into a continuous biodiversity
          survey. The list of species heard can be published openly while the detailed record,
          with times, counts, and the sensor readings from the same moment, stays behind access
          control. Section {byId['community-research'].n} covers who gets to query that.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          A forecast covers a region. A station covers a site, and the difference between the two
          is what a person standing there actually experiences. Years of readings from one place
          also answer questions its operator never thought to ask, so the record earns its
          keeping long after the day it was written.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={mark('openSenseMap')} />
          <Mark item={cwop} />
          <Mark item={ttn} />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="smart-security">
        <Fragment set:html={head('smart-security')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Cameras recorded and analyzed on local hardware keep footage of a property on the
          property, and keep working when the connection does not.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Network cameras publish a continuous video stream that any recorder on the same network
          can read, using open protocols for both the video and the discovery of the cameras
          themselves. A recorder writes the streams to the storage pool in segments.
        </p>
        <p>
          Running a detection model over the frames is what makes those recordings searchable. An
          alert fires when a person, a vehicle, or an animal is actually in frame. Plain motion
          detection fires on any pixel that changes, which in wind and rain means all night. A
          small dedicated inference accelerator handles the detection so the graphics cards stay
          free for other work.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          The consumer version of this uploads everything to a manufacturer and charges a monthly
          fee for access to your own recordings, so the cameras stop being useful the moment the
          connection or the company does. Keeping the cameras, the recorder, and the model on one
          local network puts retention in the owner's hands and means no footage leaves without a
          deliberate decision to send it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Detections handled locally compose with everything else here. An alert can go out over
          the mesh to a handheld with no cell service in range, and it can be read next to what
          the weather station and the microphone recorded at that same moment.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="ai-compute">
        <Fragment set:html={head('ai-compute')} />
        <p class="lead">
          A machine you own cannot be suspended, repriced, or reclaimed on somebody else's
          schedule. Powering it from your own generation extends that to the electricity it runs
          on.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Graphics cards do the heavy arithmetic that running a model requires. A small
          single-board computer handles the housekeeping and the sensor plumbing, which needs
          very little power and no graphics card at all.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={mark('NVIDIA')} />
          <Mark item={mark('AMD')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Raspberry Pi')} />
        </div>
        <p>
          The software counts as much as the hardware. An operating system and a serving stack
          whose source can be read let an operator follow a request all the way to its answer,
          fix what breaks, and keep the system running after the people who wrote it have moved
          on. Behavior that can be read does not have to be taken on trust.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={mark('Linux')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Docker')} />
          <Mark item={mark('vLLM')} />
        </div>
        <h3>Power</h3>
        <p>
          Owning a machine that draws from a single utility connection trades a cloud provider's
          terms for a utility's. Generation and storage therefore belong inside the system, owned
          the way a disk is owned. Solar, wind, and hydro each supply power nobody bills for and
          nobody can switch off remotely. Generators running on biogas or biodiesel cover the
          days none of the three deliver. A battery bank holds the surplus for the hours when the
          sun is down and the wind is still.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Owning both the machine and its power changes how deep a failure goes. A grid outage
          becomes a change somewhere in the middle of the stack instead of an event that takes
          everything off the air, and the compute above it carries on answering.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="nas">
        <Fragment set:html={head('nas')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Network attached storage puts every disk behind one file service, so all the machines
          on the network work against a single pool that checks its own data for corruption.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          A NAS is a machine whose only job is disks. Several drives are combined into one array,
          a filesystem spans the whole thing, and standard protocols hand it to clients: one for
          desktops, one for Unix hosts, and an object storage interface for software that expects
          to talk to storage the way cloud services do.
        </p>
        <p>
          Redundancy means the pool keeps serving through the loss of a drive and rebuilds onto a
          replacement. Redundancy is not backup. A file deleted on a redundant array is deleted
          on every disk in it at once. Snapshots, and copies kept somewhere else, are what
          recover a mistake or a site that has burned down.
        </p>
        <h3>Integrity</h3>
        <p>
          For a record meant to be read years from now, correctness matters more than capacity.
          Disks occasionally return data that differs from what was written, without reporting an
          error. A checksumming filesystem stores a fingerprint of every block it writes and
          checks it on every read, so corruption is caught instead of served. A periodic pass
          over the whole pool rebuilds any bad block from a good copy and writes it back. That is
          the difference between a disk and an archive.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Everything else here produces data that has to land somewhere. Model files
          are large and must not change once chosen. Sensor readings and species identifications
          accumulate continuously. Camera recordings arrive around the clock. One checked pool
          holds all of it, and it is the one thing here worth backing up.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="open-weights">
        <Fragment set:html={head('open-weights')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Open weight models are published as files anyone can download and run on their own
          hardware. A model chosen today still answers the same way years from now, because
          nobody else can change it or take it back.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          A model is a very large table of numbers. Training adjusts those numbers over months of
          computation, and what comes out is a file. Running the model is arithmetic over that
          file. A closed model stays on a company's servers, reachable only through an interface
          that accepts questions and returns answers.
        </p>
        <p>
          Open weights are not the same as open source. The Open Source Initiative's definition
          of open source AI also asks for the training code and enough information about the
          training data to rebuild an equivalent system. Most weight releases publish neither,
          and their licenses differ enough that reading them matters.
        </p>
        <h3>What the file makes possible</h3>
        <p>
          Three properties follow from having the file. A hosted model can be updated or retired
          at any time, and a published result can stop reproducing with no change at the
          researcher's end, while a file on local disk holds still and can be cited exactly and
          rerun later. A question asked of a local model never leaves the machine, and work
          carried out under an ethics approval often requires exactly that. A downloaded file
          also cannot be recalled by anyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Weights arrive without the machinery to run them. Owned compute and the power beneath
          it are that machinery.
        </p>
        <h3>What it does here</h3>
        <p>
          A model running locally takes on work that would otherwise wait for a person. It
          watches the radio links and reports behavior that looks wrong. It does the listening
          behind the species list. It combines station measurements with the most recent
          satellite pass into a forecast for one specific site, which no regional forecast
          attempts.
        </p>
        <p>
          It can also appear as a contact on the mesh, so anyone in radio range messages it the
          way they would message a person, with no cell service and no billed link anywhere in
          the path. Compute of this kind stays in one place, and radio is what carries its
          answers out to where the people are.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          The compute in section {byId['ai-compute'].n} is only as durable as what it runs. A
          published file is the one form of model that stays available on the operator's terms
          rather than the publisher's.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={mark('Hugging Face')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Llama')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Qwen')} />
          <Mark item={mark('DeepSeek')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Mistral')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Gemma')} />
          <Mark item={mark('OpenMed')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Meshtastic')} />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="vpn">
        <Fragment set:html={head('vpn')} />
        <p class="lead">
          A virtual private network carries traffic inside an encrypted tunnel between two
          machines, so every network in between sees the tunnel and not what it holds. It is how
          a machine at home is reached from outside without opening a single port to the
          internet.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          The two ends prove their identity to each other, agree on keys, and then wrap every
          packet inside another packet addressed to the far end. Anything carrying that traffic
          sees only the outer packet.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same mechanism does two jobs. It reaches a private machine from anywhere without
          exposing anything to the open internet. And it moves the point where traffic enters the
          public internet, so the local network and the access provider see encrypted packets to
          one address instead of a record of everywhere the machine went. The modern protocols
          are small enough to read from end to end, so claims about how a tunnel behaves can be
          checked rather than believed.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Running the far end on owned hardware means the tunnel terminates on a machine with a
          known configuration and no logging policy to take on faith. Traffic leaves under an
          address that belongs to the operator. That makes a tunnel an instrument of
          confidentiality and control. Anonymity is a different property, and the next section is
          about the tool for it.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={headscale} />
          <Mark item={mark('WireGuard')} />
          <Mark item={mark('OpenVPN')} />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="tor">
        <Fragment set:html={head('tor')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Tor sends traffic through three relays run by three different parties, so no single one
          of them knows both who is connecting and where the connection is going. Its onion
          services also make a machine reachable with no public address at all.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          The software wraps each packet in three layers of encryption and sends it along a path
          it picked itself. The first relay knows the sender's address but not the destination.
          The middle relay knows neither. The last relay knows the destination but not the
          sender. Each one removes a single layer and can read nothing except where to send the
          packet next.
        </p>
        <p>
          Anonymity here comes from traffic crossing many operators who do not pool what they
          see, so the network gets stronger as more independent people run relays.
        </p>
        <h3>Onion services</h3>
        <p>
          A service published at an onion address is reached through the network itself. It needs
          no public address, no forwarded port, and no entry in the naming system. That works
          from behind the kind of shared address described in section
          {' '}{byId['dynamic-dns'].n}, and it works without asking any provider for permission.
          The address is derived from the service's own key, so arriving at the right address and
          verifying it are the same act.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          A machine with bandwidth to spare can carry traffic for the network as a relay or a
          bridge. That is the ordinary way to add capacity to it, and it is capacity the whole
          network shares.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="stun-server">
        <Fragment set:html={head('stun-server')} />
        <p class="lead">
          A STUN server tells a machine behind a home router what its address looks like from
          the outside. That is the first step in connecting two machines that are both behind
          routers, and hosting one removes the last outside party from the arrangement.
        </p>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          Home routers rewrite addresses. A machine behind one sees only a private address, while
          the packets it sends leave under a public address and port number the router picked.
          The machine has no way to learn what those are on its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          A STUN server answers exactly that question. A machine sends it a packet, and the reply
          reports the address and port the server saw the packet arrive from. Once two machines
          have each learned their own, they trade the details over any channel and then send to
          each other at the same moment, so each router treats the incoming packet as an answer
          to its own outbound one. This is called hole punching, and it is how two machines build
          a direct connection instead of routing everything through somebody's relay.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some routers assign ports unpredictably and hole punching fails against them. A TURN
          server relays the traffic in those cases, and one open-source server commonly provides
          both roles.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Networks built on this pattern normally point at servers belonging to whoever wrote the
          software, which is one company holding the introductions for every connection. Hosting
          the coordinating server and its relay puts the whole path on owned hardware, from
          learning an address through to relaying when learning one is not enough.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={coturn} />
          <Mark item={headscale} />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="dynamic-dns">
        <Fragment set:html={head('dynamic-dns')} />
        <p class="lead">
          The naming system turns names into addresses, and both halves of it can be run at
          home. One keeps your own name pointed at an address that keeps changing. The other
          looks up everyone else's names directly, so no outside company sees what the household
          is reaching for.
        </p>
        <h3>Naming your own address</h3>
        <p>
          Residential connections take their public address from a pool and can be handed a
          different one after any reconnection. A fixed name solves that, as long as something
          updates the name whenever the address moves. The update method is standardized and
          authenticated with a shared key, so a short script can rewrite a single record on the
          server that holds the name. Every widely used name server accepts updates this way.
        </p>
        <p>
          The server holding the name has to sit at an address that does not move, which the home
          connection does not. It therefore runs elsewhere: a second site, a peer hosting the
          record reciprocally, or a small rented machine. Handing one subdomain to it keeps the
          main domain at the registrar while the changing records stay under owned control.
        </p>
        <p>
          A name is not a path. Many residential, cellular, and satellite providers put a large
          number of customers behind one shared public address, and no incoming connection can
          reach any single customer through it. The name resolves correctly to an address nobody
          can connect to. Reachability comes instead from the tunnels, onion services, and relays
          above, all of which open their connections outward, and the name then points at that
          endpoint rather than at the home connection.
        </p>
        <h3>Resolving everyone else's</h3>
        <p>
          The other half runs in the opposite direction. Every connection any device makes starts
          by looking up a name, and by default that lookup goes to a resolver belonging to an
          internet provider or a large company, which sees every site the household visits.
        </p>
        <p>
          A resolver running at home asks the root servers, then the servers for the top-level
          domain, then the servers holding the name itself. It also verifies the cryptographic
          signatures published alongside a growing share of records, which is how a forged answer
          is detected. Filtering lists apply at this layer too, so unwanted domains are refused
          for every device on the network at once.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Names are the first thing every connection touches, in both directions. Running both
          halves keeps the household's lookups inside the house and keeps its own name under its
          operator's control.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={knot} />
          <Mark item={powerdns} />
          <Mark item={bind} />
          <Mark item={unbound} />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="marks" id="community-research">
        <Fragment set:html={head('community-research')} />
        <p class="lead">
          Owned infrastructure comes with surplus, and surplus is what can be given away. That
          headroom is what students and researchers nearby get to work on.
        </p>
        <h3>Where the surplus comes from</h3>
        <p>
          Renting sizes a resource to exactly what is paid for. Owning means buying whole units:
          more processors than tonight's job needs, more disk than this year's data fills, more
          power than the load draws at noon. A rented seat is metered to one person and cannot be
          handed to anybody, so an operator paying for exactly their own usage has nothing to
          offer even when they would like to. An owned machine has capacity sitting idle between
          jobs, and pointing it at somebody else's work costs its owner none of their own.
        </p>
        <h3>What the compute is used for</h3>
        <p>
          Research work here falls into three kinds. The kind of work decides whether a tool
          helps, which matters more than which company built it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Chat and search interfaces explore an idea, sort through a stack of papers, or check a
          hypothesis without writing retrieval code for every question. Grounding is what makes
          them useful for research. An answer tied to named sources can be followed and cited. An
          answer drawn from general training has to be verified by hand.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <AltPair pair={pair('Open WebUI')} />
          <AltPair pair={pair('SearXNG')} />
          <AltPair pair={pair('Onyx')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Cherry Studio')} />
        </div>
        <p>
          Agentic coding tools let a lab build and maintain a pipeline without an engineering
          team. The agent reads the code, makes a change, runs the tests, and goes round again,
          which is the loop a person works through. These tools sit on top of a model instead of
          being tied to one, so they can be pointed at whichever model suits the job.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <AltPair pair={pair('Cline')} />
          <AltPair pair={pair('OpenHands')} />
          <AltPair pair={pair('Goose')} />
          <Mark item={mark('opencode')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Kilo Code')} />
        </div>
        <p>
          When no existing tool fits, the same pieces recur for building one: a notebook to
          prototype in, a framework for wiring up a retrieval or agent pipeline, and a simple
          interface for handing the result to somebody who does not read code. Scheduling turns a
          one-off script into a job that runs on its own. Tracing shows which step in a chain of
          model calls produced a wrong answer.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <Mark item={mark('Jupyter')} />
          <Mark item={mark('LangChain')} />
          <Mark item={mark('LlamaIndex')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Haystack')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Gradio')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Streamlit')} />
          <Mark item={mark('Dify')} />
          <AltPair pair={pair('n8n')} />
          <AltPair pair={pair('Langfuse')} />
        </div>
        <h3>The record, and who can query it</h3>
        <p>
          The data these instruments collect is abundant in the same way as the compute. An
          interface over everything they sense turns a log kept for one person into something
          many people can query, and the collecting was happening anyway. One site is a small
          sample. A site that has been listening for years, alongside others doing the same,
          answers questions its operator would never think to ask. A public page shows the
          summary, and the interface answers the detail beneath it.
        </p>
        <ul class="log-list api-list">
          <li><span>Station readings</span><b>temperature, humidity, pressure, wind</b></li>
          <li><span>Acoustic identifications</span><b>species, time, confidence</b></li>
          <li><span>Local forecasts</span><b>derived locally</b></li>
          <li><span>Satellite imagery</span><b>visible, infrared, water vapor</b></li>
          <li><span>Contact log</span><b>aircraft, vessels, passes overhead</b></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Access begins by invitation, which is how an operator introduces people to the
          instrument properly.
        </p>
        <h3>Keeping the code with the data</h3>
        <p>
          A result reproduces from three things: the data, the model, and the code that combined
          them. Choosing a model file settles only the second. If the code lives on a platform
          that can lock an account or remove a repository, the record has a hole in it that shows
          up only when somebody tries to rerun the work years later. Institutional accounts are
          temporary in any case. Students lose them at graduation, researchers lose them on
          moving, and work held inside an account leaves with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Self-hosted code forges are mature, so keeping the history next to the compute is a
          configuration decision. Choosing between them is a question of governance. Forgejo
          exists because Gitea's trademarks and operations were transferred to a private company
          in 2022, and the maintainers forked the project under a nonprofit rather than follow
          it.
        </p>
        <div class="mark-row">
          <AltPair pair={pair('Forgejo')} openTitle="the self-hosted alternative" />
        </div>
        <h3>Why it belongs</h3>
        <p>
          Whoever owns the compute decides what gets built on it. An owner with room to spare can
          hand that decision to the people doing the work, which is the whole reason to build the
          surplus in the first place.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>

  <script>
    // Ambient readouts for the field diagram. Values drift inside realistic bands.
    import { solarPct, batt as battAt } from '../data/solarday.js';

    const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id)!;
    const clamp = (v: number, lo: number, hi: number) => Math.min(hi, Math.max(lo, v));
    const pick = <T,>(a: T[]) => a[Math.floor(Math.random() * a.length)];

    const WX_STATES = ['CLEAR', 'MARINE LAYER', 'OVERCAST', 'LT RAIN'];
    const WX_DIRS = ['N', 'NNW', 'NW', 'WNW', 'W', 'SW'];
    const randWx = () => ({ state: pick(WX_STATES), kt: 4 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 10), dir: pick(WX_DIRS) });

    let temp = 43 + Math.random() * 10;
    let mesh = 7 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 3);
    let wx = randWx();

    // Solar and battery come off the shared day model, the same one the
    // resilience diagram draws in full; the readout drift is added here so the
    // drawn curves stay clean.
    const hourNow = () => new Date().getHours() + new Date().getMinutes() / 60;

    const solar = () => {
      const s = solarPct(hourNow());
      return s === 0 ? 0 : Math.max(0, Math.round(s + (Math.random() * 6 - 3)));
    };

    const batt = () => {
      const b = battAt(hourNow());
      return b.mode === 'FLOAT' ? { pct: b.pct - Math.random(), mode: b.mode } : b;
    };

    const render = (s: number) => {
      const b = batt();
      el('m-solar').textContent = `${s}%`;
      el('m-batt').textContent = `${Math.round(b.pct)}% ${b.mode}`;
      el('m-temp').textContent = `${temp.toFixed(1)}°C`;
      el('m-wx').textContent = `${wx.state} · ${wx.kt}KT ${wx.dir}`;
      el('m-mesh').textContent = `${mesh} HEARD`;
    };

    const tick = () => {
      temp = clamp(temp + (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.9, 37, 68);
      if (Math.random() < 0.07) mesh = clamp(mesh + (Math.random() < 0.5 ? -1 : 1), 7, 10);
      if (Math.random() < 0.04) wx = randWx();
      render(solar());
    };

    render(solar());

    // Beacon emissions. Each emitter fires at its protocol's real report interval
    // (data-every, seconds): ADS-B ident 5s, AIS class A underway 10s, satellite
    // downlink carrier 2s. A ring is parked at the emitter's position at the moment
    // of emission (it does not travel with the vehicle), expands to the window edges
    // in 6s, and the node color-pulses when the front covers the node's distance.
    const map = document.querySelector<SVGSVGElement>('.map')!;
    const nodeGlyph = el('node-glyph');
    const waves = [...document.querySelectorAll<SVGCircleElement>('.wave')];
    const rings = [...document.querySelectorAll<SVGCircleElement>('.ring')];
    let ringIdx = 0;
    let waveIdx = 0;
    let txTimer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;

    const mapCoords = (elm: Element) => {
      const r = elm.getBoundingClientRect();
      const m = map.getBoundingClientRect();
      const s = Math.max(m.width / 1600, m.height / 1000);
      return [
        (r.left + r.width / 2 - m.left - (m.width - 1600 * s) / 2) / s,
        (r.top + r.height / 2 - m.top - (m.height - 1000 * s) / 2) / s,
      ];
    };

    const emit = (x: number, y: number) => {
      const reach = Math.max(
        Math.hypot(x, y), Math.hypot(1600 - x, y),
        Math.hypot(x, 1000 - y), Math.hypot(1600 - x, 1000 - y),
      );
      const ring = rings[ringIdx++ % rings.length];
      ring.setAttribute('cx', x.toFixed(1));
      ring.setAttribute('cy', y.toFixed(1));
      ring.setAttribute('r', Math.ceil(reach).toString());
      ring.classList.remove('live');
      void ring.getBoundingClientRect(); // restart the one-shot animation
      ring.classList.add('live');
      const travelMs = (Math.hypot(x - 640, y - 420) / (reach / 6)) * 1000;
      setTimeout(() => {
        nodeGlyph.classList.add('rx');
        setTimeout(() => nodeGlyph.classList.remove('rx'), 450);
        // reply beacon, one per receipt; pooled so concurrent replies overlap
        const wave = waves[waveIdx++ % waves.length];
        wave.classList.remove('live');
        void wave.getBoundingClientRect();
        wave.classList.add('live');
        // antenna waves key up briefly per reply, the same ~2s burst as the
        // stations; receipts arrive every few seconds, so a longer window would
        // chain into looking permanently on
        nodeGlyph.classList.add('tx');
        clearTimeout(txTimer);
        txTimer = setTimeout(() => nodeGlyph.classList.remove('tx'), 2000);
      }, travelMs);
    };

    // field log modal: native dialog handles focus trap and Escape
    const dlg = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('.fieldlog')!;
    el('birds-btn').addEventListener('click', () => dlg.showModal());
    dlg.querySelector('.log-close')!.addEventListener('click', () => dlg.close());
    dlg.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      if (e.target === dlg) dlg.close(); // backdrop click
    });

    const emitters = [...document.querySelectorAll<SVGElement>('[data-every]')].map((elm, i) => ({
      elm,
      every: Number(elm.dataset.every) * 1000,
      next: performance.now() + 1500 + i * 2200,
    }));

    if (!matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
      // one contact keys up right away, so the map isn't silent on load. Planes
      // and satellites use a negative animation-delay to park mid-cycle (so a
      // reduced-motion freeze doesn't strand them mid-map), and that parked spot
      // is usually off the 1600x1000 viewBox by design; only fire from whichever
      // contacts are actually on the map right now.
      const onMap = emitters
        .map((e) => ({ e, xy: mapCoords(e.elm) }))
        .filter(({ xy: [x, y] }) => x > 0 && x < 1600 && y > 0 && y < 1000);
      if (onMap.length) {
        const { e, xy } = onMap[Math.floor(Math.random() * onMap.length)];
        emit(...xy);
        e.next = performance.now() + e.every;
      }

      setInterval(tick, 2500);
      setInterval(() => {
        const now = performance.now();
        for (const e of emitters) {
          if (now < e.next) continue;
          e.next = now + e.every;
          const [x, y] = mapCoords(e.elm);
          if (x > 0 && x < 1600 && y > 0 && y < 1000) emit(x, y);
        }
      }, 500);

      // a click on the map keys up a beacon from that spot, same as any contact
      document.querySelector('.fieldmap')!.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
        if ((e.target as Element).closest('a, button')) return;
        const m = map.getBoundingClientRect();
        const s = Math.max(m.width / 1600, m.height / 1000);
        const x = (e.clientX - m.left - (m.width - 1600 * s) / 2) / s;
        const y = (e.clientY - m.top - (m.height - 1000 * s) / 2) / s;
        if (x > 0 && x < 1600 && y > 0 && y < 1000) emit(x, y);
      });
    }

    // ---- TOC scroll-spy ---------------------------------------------------
    // ponytail: IntersectionObserver rather than a scroll handler; the band is a
    // reading zone near the top of the viewport, so the row that lights is the
    // section being read rather than the one merely on screen.
    const tocLinks = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.components a[href^="#"]')];
    const tocById = new Map(tocLinks.map((a) => [a.getAttribute('href')!.slice(1), a]));
    const spy = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const e of entries) {
          if (!e.isIntersecting) continue;
          const hit = tocById.get(e.target.id);
          for (const a of tocLinks) {
            a.classList.toggle('active', a === hit);
            if (a === hit) a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'true');
            else a.removeAttribute('aria-current');
          }
        }
      },
      { rootMargin: '-10% 0px -70% 0px' },
    );
    for (const id of tocById.keys()) {
      const s = document.getElementById(id);
      if (s) spy.observe(s);
    }
  </script>

  <script define:vars={{ meshNodes, mapMeshNodes, satNode, satTracks, satGeom, satRingR, SAT_KM_PER_UNIT }}>
    // Mesh traffic engine, driving both the mesh-radio diagram and the field
    // map. A random start node keys up, the packet rides each wave front hop by
    // hop along the most direct route (Dijkstra over link distance), every
    // neighbor flashes when the front crosses it, only the next relay keys up
    // again (managed flooding: the rest heard it covered and stay quiet), and
    // the destination just flashes. Contacts message contacts on the private
    // channel, preferring my network's relays; strangers message strangers on
    // the public one, relaying through anyone. Receiving is silent;
    // transmitting is not.
    //
    // On the map the expanding-circle layer is switched off: circles there are
    // the beacon vocabulary (.wave / .ring), so mesh transmissions show only as
    // keyed wave arcs, the packet, flashes and key badges. Everything else is
    // shared, so the two scenes stay one system.
    const meshTraffic = (root, nodes, { range, circles, gapMin, gapJitter }) => {
      if (!root) return;
      const msPerUnit = 950 / range; // front speed: full range in 0.95s, matches mesh-ping
      const glyphs = nodes.map((_, i) => root.querySelector(`.glyph[data-mesh="${i}"]`));
      const pulses = circles ? [...root.querySelectorAll('.pulse')] : [];
      const packet = root.querySelector('.packet');
      const keys = [...root.querySelectorAll('.key')];
      if (glyphs.some((g) => !g) || !packet || keys.length < 2) return;
      let pulseIdx = 0;

      const dist = (a, b) => Math.hypot(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y);
      const mine = (n) => !!(n.contact || n.mine);
      const nbrs = nodes.map((a) =>
        nodes.flatMap((b, j) => (a !== b && dist(a, b) <= range ? [j] : [])),
      );

      // Dijkstra over link distance: the most direct route across the mesh.
      // private traffic prefers my network's relays (restricted pass first);
      // if no all-contact path exists it falls back to anyone, since any node
      // repeats packets it cannot read
      const route = (src, dst, priv) => {
        const N = nodes.length;
        const cost = new Array(N).fill(Infinity);
        const prev = new Array(N).fill(-1);
        const done = new Array(N).fill(false);
        cost[src] = 0;
        for (;;) {
          let i = -1;
          for (let j = 0; j < N; j++) if (!done[j] && (i < 0 || cost[j] < cost[i])) i = j;
          if (i < 0 || cost[i] === Infinity) return null;
          if (i === dst) break;
          done[i] = true;
          for (const j of nbrs[i]) {
            if (priv && !mine(nodes[j])) continue;
            const c = cost[i] + dist(nodes[i], nodes[j]);
            if (c < cost[j]) { cost[j] = c; prev[j] = i; }
          }
        }
        const path = [dst];
        while (path[0] !== src) path.unshift(prev[path[0]]);
        return path;
      };

      // pub follows the packet's channel, not the node: my network relaying a
      // stranger packet stays grey. cleared with rx so a later beacon flash on
      // the map's node glyph doesn't inherit this exchange's channel
      const flash = (i, delay, pub) =>
        setTimeout(() => {
          glyphs[i].classList.toggle('pub', pub);
          glyphs[i].classList.add('rx');
          setTimeout(() => glyphs[i].classList.remove('rx', 'pub'), 450);
        }, delay);

      const txTimers = [];
      const keyUp = (i, priv) => {
        if (pulses.length) {
          const c = pulses[pulseIdx++ % pulses.length];
          c.setAttribute('cx', nodes[i].x);
          c.setAttribute('cy', nodes[i].y);
          // accent only when my network transmits its own private traffic
          c.classList.toggle('pub', !(priv && mine(nodes[i])));
          c.classList.remove('live');
          void c.getBoundingClientRect(); // restart the one-shot animation
          c.classList.add('live');
        }
        // the glyph's wave arcs show only for the transmit window
        glyphs[i].classList.add('tx');
        clearTimeout(txTimers[i]);
        txTimers[i] = setTimeout(() => glyphs[i].classList.remove('tx'), 950);
      };

      const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));

      const glide = (a, b, ms) =>
        new Promise((done) => {
          const t0 = performance.now();
          const step = (t) => {
            const k = Math.min(1, (t - t0) / ms);
            packet.setAttribute('cx', a.x + (b.x - a.x) * k);
            packet.setAttribute('cy', a.y + (b.y - a.y) * k);
            k < 1 ? requestAnimationFrame(step) : done();
          };
          requestAnimationFrame(step);
        });

      (async () => {
        for (;;) {
          // contacts talk to contacts, strangers to strangers; relays are anyone
          const src = Math.floor(Math.random() * nodes.length);
          const peers = nodes.flatMap((n, j) => (j !== src && mine(n) === mine(nodes[src]) ? [j] : []));
          if (!peers.length) { await sleep(gapMin); continue; }
          const dst = peers[Math.floor(Math.random() * peers.length)];
          const priv = mine(nodes[src]);
          const path = route(src, dst, priv) ?? route(src, dst, false);
          if (!path) { await sleep(gapMin); continue; }
          packet.style.fill = priv ? 'var(--accent)' : 'var(--text-mute)';
          packet.setAttribute('cx', nodes[src].x);
          packet.setAttribute('cy', nodes[src].y);
          packet.classList.add('live');
          // key badges mark sender and receiver for the exchange's duration
          [src, dst].forEach((n, k) => {
            keys[k].setAttribute('x', nodes[n].x - 30);
            keys[k].setAttribute('y', nodes[n].y - 30);
            keys[k].classList.toggle('pub', !priv);
            keys[k].classList.add('live');
          });
          for (let k = 0; k < path.length - 1; k++) {
            const a = nodes[path[k]];
            keyUp(path[k], priv); // transmit / rebroadcast
            flash(path[k], 0, !priv);
            // every radio in range hears the broadcast as the front crosses it
            for (const j of nbrs[path[k]]) flash(j, dist(a, nodes[j]) * msPerUnit, !priv);
            await glide(a, nodes[path[k + 1]], dist(a, nodes[path[k + 1]]) * msPerUnit);
          }
          packet.classList.remove('live'); // destination receives: flash only, no transmit
          keys.forEach((k) => k.classList.remove('live'));
          await sleep(gapMin + Math.random() * gapJitter);
        }
      })();
    };

    // Satellite downlink engine (03). One pass at a time: a satellite glides its
    // whole track at orbital speed over COMPRESSION, broadcasting the entire
    // way, because that is what these things do whether or not anyone is
    // listening. A pooled carrier is parked wherever it currently is and fired
    // out to the footprint radius; the front reaches the node only if the node
    // was inside that footprint, after a delay taken from the drawn distance.
    // Scan lines land for as long as the satellite is in range, scaled so the
    // longest pass in the roster is exactly the one that fills the picture,
    // which leaves the grazing pass short without anything special-casing it.
    // Nothing in here ever transmits: no keyUp, no --sig, no pulse from the node.
    const satPass = (root, node, tracks, geom, ringR, kmPerUnit) => {
      if (!root) return;
      const sats = tracks.map((_, i) => root.querySelector(`.glyph[data-sat="${i}"]`));
      const swaths = tracks.map((_, i) => root.querySelector(`[data-swath="${i}"]`));
      const nodeGlyph = root.querySelector('.glyph[data-sat="node"]');
      const carriers = [...root.querySelectorAll('.carrier')];
      const still = root.querySelector('.carrier-static');
      if (sats.some((s) => !s) || swaths.some((s) => !s)) return;
      if (!nodeGlyph || !carriers.length) return;

      const COMPRESSION = 60; // a second on screen is a minute overhead
      const SAT_KMS = 7.7; // LEO ground speed
      const FRONT_MS = 2000; // a carrier covers the footprint radius in this long.
      // paired with sat-carrier's 2s in the style block; change the two together.
      // it is a legibility speed, not compressed light: real propagation across
      // this frame is microseconds. what geometry decides is which fronts reach
      // the node at all, and in what order.
      const CARRIER_EVERY_MS = 1000; // the broadcast is continuous; this is only
      // how often we draw a front of it
      const PASS_GAP_MS = 3000; // between passes. compressed harder than the pass
      // itself, so the diagram is not mostly an empty sky
      const RX_MS = 450; // same receive flash the mesh diagram uses
      const speed = (SAT_KMS * COMPRESSION) / kmPerUnit; // viewBox units per second

      const at = (f, k) => ({ x: k.x1 + (k.x2 - k.x1) * f, y: k.y1 + (k.y2 - k.y1) * f });
      const place = (g, p) => {
        g.setAttribute('x', p.x - 13);
        g.setAttribute('y', p.y - 13);
      };
      let carrierIdx = 0;
      let rxTimer;
      const fire = (p) => {
        const c = carriers[carrierIdx++ % carriers.length];
        c.setAttribute('cx', p.x);
        c.setAttribute('cy', p.y);
        c.classList.remove('live');
        void c.getBoundingClientRect(); // restart the one-shot animation
        c.classList.add('live');
        const d = Math.hypot(p.x - node.x, p.y - node.y);
        if (d > ringR) return; // out of footprint: the front dies before it arrives
        setTimeout(() => {
          // an open broadcast is a public payload, so the node darkens within the
          // greys and never borrows accent, exactly as it does relaying a
          // stranger's packet on the mesh
          nodeGlyph.classList.add('rx', 'pub');
          clearTimeout(rxTimer);
          rxTimer = setTimeout(() => nodeGlyph.classList.remove('rx', 'pub'), RX_MS);
        }, (d / ringR) * FRONT_MS);
      };

      const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));

      const run = (i) =>
        new Promise((done) => {
          const k = tracks[i];
          const { len } = geom[i];
          const ms = (len / speed) * 1000;
          const t0 = performance.now();
          let nextCarrier = 0;
          const step = (now) => {
            const elapsed = now - t0;
            const f = Math.min(1, elapsed / ms);
            const p = at(f, k);
            place(sats[i], p);
            if (elapsed >= nextCarrier) {
              fire(p);
              nextCarrier = elapsed + CARRIER_EVERY_MS; // self-correcting: a
              // backgrounded tab resumes without firing a burst
            }
            // the swath grows behind the satellite for the whole pass: the
            // imager sweeps continuously, whether or not this node is in a
            // position to hear the result. what the node actually got is the
            // stricter claim, and fire() keeps it gated on the ring.
            swaths[i].setAttribute('width', f * len);
            f < 1 ? requestAnimationFrame(step) : done();
          };
          requestAnimationFrame(step);
        });

      (async () => {
        // the template ships a static frame for reduced motion: all four
        // satellites parked with the ground each has swept behind it, and a
        // carrier caught mid-expansion. moving means clearing all of it first.
        sats.forEach((s) => s.classList.add('off'));
        still?.classList.add('off');
        swaths.forEach((s) => {
          s.setAttribute('width', 0);
          s.classList.remove('stale');
        });
        for (let i = 0; ; i = (i + 1) % tracks.length) {
          // this orbit is back over the same ground, so its old strip is wiped
          // and laid down again from nothing
          swaths[i].setAttribute('width', 0);
          swaths[i].classList.remove('stale');
          place(sats[i], at(0, tracks[i]));
          sats[i].classList.remove('off');
          await run(i);
          sats[i].classList.add('off');
          // the strip starts ageing the instant it is complete and fades evenly
          // for the whole interval until this same orbit returns to redraw it,
          // so at any moment the four strips are at four different ages
          swaths[i].classList.add('stale');
          await sleep(PASS_GAP_MS);
        }
      })();
    };

    if (!matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
      meshTraffic(document.querySelector('.meshdiagram'), meshNodes, {
        range: 290, circles: true, gapMin: 500, gapJitter: 0,
      });
      // ambient on the map: one exchange every 2-4s, alongside the beacons
      meshTraffic(document.querySelector('.fieldmap'), mapMeshNodes, {
        range: 300, circles: false, gapMin: 2000, gapJitter: 2000,
      });
      satPass(document.querySelector('.satdiagram'), satNode, satTracks, satGeom, satRingR, SAT_KM_PER_UNIT);
    }
  </script>

  <style>
    /* ---- fieldmap hero ----------------------------------------------------
       Full-bleed stage, garage recipe: opaque bg + its own dot grid, because
       the fixed body::before grid doesn't scroll and would double-print. */
    .fieldmap {
      position: relative;
      z-index: 1;
      cursor: crosshair;
      height: calc(100vh - 60px);
      height: calc(100dvh - 60px);
      border-top: 0;
      /* the site's section rule puts the rule on top; the map's neighbour also
         opts out of that, so close the map off with its own bottom edge */
      border-bottom: var(--border-w) solid var(--border);
      padding: 0;
      overflow: hidden;
      background-color: var(--bg);
      background-image: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.09) 1px, transparent 1px);
      background-size: 16px 16px;
    }
    .map {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
    }
    .graticule line {
      stroke: var(--border);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .tick rect {
      fill: var(--ink);
    }
    .tick text,
    .tag {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 13px;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      fill: var(--text-mute);
    }
    .contours path {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--border-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .contours path.index {
      stroke: var(--stone);
      stroke-width: 1.3;
    }
    .coast .shore {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--field-grey);
      stroke-width: 1.6;
    }
    /* ponytail: wide soft stroke suggests water instead of a filled sea polygon */
    .coast .shore-glow {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--field-grey);
      stroke-width: 10;
      opacity: 0.07;
    }
    .sea-label {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 20px;
      letter-spacing: 0.35em;
      fill: var(--text-mute);
      opacity: 0.55;
    }

    /* signal choreography: front expands 6s of an 8s cycle; each relay pings as
       the front passes (--recv = distance / front speed) and the node flashes
       when the reply makes it back (2x --recv, baked into nodeflash offsets) */
    .wave {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
      transform-box: fill-box;
      transform-origin: center;
      opacity: 0;
    }
    .wave.live {
      animation: wave 6s linear;
    }
    @keyframes wave {
      0% { transform: scale(0.02); opacity: 0.75; }
      90% { opacity: 0.1; }
      100% { transform: scale(1); opacity: 0; }
    }
    .glyph {
      color: var(--text-mute);
      overflow: visible;
    }
    .node .glyph {
      color: var(--text);
      transition: color 0.15s;
    }
    /* set by the script the moment a beacon front reaches the node */
    .node .glyph.rx {
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    /* beacon rings: fixed epicenter, expand to the window edges over 6s (one-shot,
       restarted by the script each emission) */
    .ring {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--stone);
      stroke-width: 1.25;
      vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
      transform-box: fill-box;
      transform-origin: center;
      opacity: 0;
    }
    .ring.live {
      animation: ringout 6s linear;
    }
    @keyframes ringout {
      0% { transform: scale(0.02); opacity: 0.4; }
      85% { opacity: 0.05; }
      100% { transform: scale(1); opacity: 0; }
    }
    /* map mesh nodes follow the diagram's system: contacts darker, arcs only
       while transmitting (no --sig here), sun badge marks my network */
    .mesh .glyph {
      color: var(--ash);
      transition: color 0.15s;
    }
    .mesh.contact .glyph {
      color: var(--field-grey);
    }
    .mesh .sun {
      color: var(--text-mute);
      overflow: visible;
    }
    .mesh .tag {
      opacity: 0.8;
    }
    /* an acoustic bird ID at the node: same hard on/off idiom as the stations,
       on a long cycle so it reads as occasional rather than a blinker */
    .bird {
      color: var(--text-mute);
      opacity: 0;
      animation: birdcall 26s linear infinite;
    }
    @keyframes birdcall {
      0%, 8% { opacity: 1; }
      8.5%, 100% { opacity: 0; }
    }
    /* hard on/off, no fade: a station darkens and raises its wave marks for the
       ~2s it is keyed up per 9s cycle, staggered by --pd */
    .peer .glyph {
      animation: peerkey 9s linear infinite;
      animation-delay: var(--pd);
    }
    @keyframes peerkey {
      0%, 22% { color: var(--text); --sig: 1; }
      22.5%, 100% { color: var(--text-mute); --sig: 0; }
    }
    /* .signal = the radiating-wave strokes in the node and tower defs. Selectors
       can't reach inside <use> shadow clones, but custom properties inherit
       through them, so --sig gates visibility per instance: stations during
       their keyed-up window (peerkey above), the node during the ~2s keyed
       burst of each reply (.tx, set by the script). Hidden otherwise. */
    #tower .signal,
    #node-icon .signal,
    #mesh-icon .signal {
      opacity: var(--sig, 0);
    }
    .node .glyph.tx {
      --sig: 1;
    }
    /* mesh traffic on the map, same coding as the diagram: strangers flash dark,
       my contacts accent, and anything carrying a public packet stays grey.
       the .fieldmap rule is last and more specific, so it also greys my node
       when it relays stranger traffic without touching the beacon rx flash */
    .mesh .glyph.tx {
      --sig: 1;
    }
    .mesh .glyph.rx {
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .mesh.contact .glyph.rx {
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .fieldmap .glyph.rx.pub {
      color: var(--slate-char);
    }

    .flight .trail,
    .vessel .trail {
      stroke: var(--text-mute);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 2 7;
      opacity: 0.6;
    }
    .flight use {
      color: var(--text-dim);
    }
    .vessel .glyph {
      color: var(--text-dim);
    }
    /* moving groups animate relative to a static base translate on their parent,
       so a reduced-motion freeze parks planes off-screen and boats on station */
    .flight-a {
      animation: flight-a 150s linear infinite;
      animation-delay: -145s;
    }
    @keyframes flight-a {
      0% { transform: translate(0, 0); }
      40%, 100% { transform: translate(1920px, -740px); }
    }
    .flight-b {
      animation: flight-b 180s linear infinite;
      animation-delay: -60s;
    }
    @keyframes flight-b {
      0% { transform: translate(0, 0); }
      33%, 100% { transform: translate(-1960px, 440px); }
    }
    .sat-track {
      stroke: var(--border-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-dasharray: 1 8;
      opacity: 0.7;
    }
    .sat .glyph {
      color: var(--text-dim);
    }
    .sat-a {
      animation: sat-a 300s linear infinite;
      animation-delay: -290s;
    }
    @keyframes sat-a {
      0% { transform: translate(0, 0); }
      1.8%, 100% { transform: translate(-172px, 1080px); }
    }
    .sat-b {
      animation: sat-b 420s linear infinite;
      animation-delay: -200s;
    }
    @keyframes sat-b {
      0% { transform: translate(0, 0); }
      2.1%, 100% { transform: translate(1480px, -1080px); }
    }
    .vessel-a {
      animation: vessel-a 2800s linear infinite;
    }
    @keyframes vessel-a {
      to { transform: translate(340px, 320px); }
    }
    .vessel-b {
      animation: vessel-b 3300s linear infinite;
    }
    @keyframes vessel-b {
      to { transform: translate(-310px, -280px); }
    }

    /* ---- HUD -------------------------------------------------------------- */
    .hud {
      position: absolute;
      z-index: 2;
    }
    /* the identity block lives in .hero-wrap (map section only) and sticks to the
       window top while the map scrolls; the wrapper's bottom edge then
       pushes it up, so it lands at the top of the node TOC section and leaves
       with it instead of pinning past it */
    .hero-wrap {
      position: relative;
    }
    .hud-identity {
      position: sticky;
      top: 0;
      z-index: 3;
      height: 0;
      padding-left: var(--sp-5);
      max-width: min(560px, 75vw);
      /* flex stops the eyebrow's top margin collapsing through this 0-height box:
         the content hangs sp-5 below the box in BOTH resting states, pinned under
         the site header and landed at the top of the node section. Padding on the
         box itself would put the inset above the box's bottom edge, which is the
         edge the push-out stops at, landing the text flush with the section top. */
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }
    .hud-identity .eyebrow {
      margin: var(--sp-5) 0 var(--sp-2);
      color: var(--accent-ink);
      letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    }
    .hud-identity h1 {
      font-size: var(--step-3);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    }
    .hud-metrics {
      top: var(--sp-5);
      right: var(--sp-5);
      margin: 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--sp-3);
      text-align: right;
      padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-4);
    }
    .hud-metrics::before,
    .hud-metrics::after {
      content: '';
      position: absolute;
      width: 12px;
      height: 12px;
      opacity: 0.7;
    }
    .hud-metrics::before {
      top: 0;
      left: 0;
      border-top: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
      border-left: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
    }
    .hud-metrics::after {
      bottom: 0;
      right: 0;
      border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
      border-right: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
    }
    .metric .label {
      display: block;
      font-size: var(--step--2);
    }
    .metric .m-val {
      display: block;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-weight: 700;
      font-size: var(--step-0);
      color: var(--text);
    }
    button.metric {
      background: none;
      border: 0;
      padding: 0;
      text-align: right;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    button.metric:hover .m-val {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    button.metric:focus-visible {
      outline: none;
      box-shadow: var(--ring);
    }

    /* ---- field log modal --------------------------------------------------- */
    .fieldlog {
      /* pin to the viewport, centered: Tailwind's preflight zeroes every margin
         (killing UA auto-centering), and fixed keeps it put while the page scrolls */
      position: fixed;
      inset: 0;
      margin: auto;
      height: fit-content;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      color: var(--text);
      background-color: var(--bg);
      background-image: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.09) 1px, transparent 1px);
      background-size: 16px 16px;
      border: var(--border-w) solid var(--border-strong);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      padding: var(--sp-5);
      width: min(420px, calc(100vw - 2 * var(--sp-5)));
      max-height: min(76vh, 76dvh);
      overflow-y: auto;
    }
    .fieldlog::backdrop {
      background: rgba(21, 22, 20, 0.45);
    }
    .log-head {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: var(--sp-4);
    }
    .log-close {
      background: none;
      border: 0;
      padding: 0.3rem;
      cursor: pointer;
      color: var(--text-mute);
    }
    .log-close:hover {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    .log-close:focus-visible {
      outline: none;
      box-shadow: var(--ring);
    }
    .log-close svg {
      display: block;
      width: 18px;
      height: 18px;
      fill: none;
      stroke: currentColor;
      stroke-width: 2;
      stroke-linecap: round;
    }
    .log-sec {
      margin: var(--sp-5) 0 var(--sp-2);
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    .log-sub {
      margin: var(--sp-3) 0 var(--sp-1);
      font-size: var(--step--2);
    }
    .log-list {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }
    .log-list li {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: 2ch;
      padding: 0.22em 0;
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
      color: var(--text-dim);
      border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--border);
    }
    .log-list li:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
    .log-list b {
      color: var(--text);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .log-note {
      margin: var(--sp-3) 0 0;
      font-size: var(--step--2);
    }
    /* the article's API listing borrows the field log's row treatment, so the
       data the page shows and the data it describes read as the same thing */
    .api-list {
      margin: var(--sp-4) 0 0;
      max-width: 62ch;
    }
    .hud-compass {
      bottom: var(--sp-5);
      right: var(--sp-5);
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--sp-5);
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      color: var(--text-mute);
    }
    .north {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.4ch;
    }
    .north svg,
    .hud-more svg {
      width: 18px;
      height: 18px;
      fill: none;
      stroke: currentColor;
      stroke-width: 2;
      stroke-linecap: round;
      stroke-linejoin: round;
    }
    .scale {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.8ch;
    }
    /* 5 km = 136 viewBox units; under xMidYMid slice the on-screen scale factor is
       max(100vw/1600, 100vh/1000), so this width stays exact at any window size */
    .scale-bar {
      display: inline-block;
      width: max(8.5vw, 13.6vh);
      height: 6px;
      border: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
      border-top: none;
    }
    .hud-more {
      bottom: var(--sp-4);
      left: 50%;
      margin-left: -19px;
      display: inline-flex;
      padding: 0.5rem;
      color: var(--text-mute);
      animation: bob 2.4s ease-in-out infinite;
    }
    .hud-more svg {
      width: 22px;
      height: 22px;
    }
    @keyframes bob {
      0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
      50% { transform: translateY(6px); }
    }
    .node a {
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .node a:hover .tag {
      fill: var(--accent-ink);
    }

    /* ---- the two drawings, closing the preamble ---------------------------- */
    .node-arts {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      gap: clamp(var(--sp-6), 7vw, 7rem);
      margin-top: var(--sp-7);
    }
    .node-art {
      /* width-driven here, unlike the full-height section they used to fill */
      width: min(38%, 340px);
      height: auto;
      min-width: 0;
      object-fit: contain;
    }

    /* ---- article layout: docs-style sticky TOC beside the prose ------------- */
    /* full window width: no .col cap, just the shared gutter. Line length is
       still governed per element (paragraphs cap at 62ch, diagrams at 860px),
       so widening the page widens the layout and not the prose. */
    #doc {
      display: grid;
      /* Three tracks, two of them used. The TOC track takes at least its own
         content width, so its rows never wrap, and the third track is an empty
         rail of the same width: the article then sits between two rails the way
         a docs site balances a contents column against an "on this page" one.
         Only two children exist, so the rail stays blank. */
      --doc-rail: 13rem;
      grid-template-columns:
        minmax(max-content, var(--doc-rail))
        minmax(0, 1fr)
        var(--doc-rail);
      column-gap: clamp(var(--sp-6), 5vw, var(--sp-8));
      align-items: start;
      padding: var(--sp-7) var(--pad-x) 0;
    }
    /* the diagrams are wider than the text column; min-width lets the grid track
       shrink so they scroll inside .scroller instead of stretching the page */
    .doc-body {
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .components {
      position: sticky;
      top: var(--sp-5);
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--sp-2);
      /* 16 rows plus 5 chapter labels outgrow a laptop viewport, so the rail
         scrolls inside itself rather than being clipped by the sticky box */
      max-height: calc(100vh - 2 * var(--sp-5));
      overflow-y: auto;
      scrollbar-width: thin;
    }
    .components a.active {
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .components-glyph {
      width: 44px;
      height: 44px;
      color: var(--text);
      --sig: 1; /* static diagram context: show the antenna waves */
      margin-bottom: var(--sp-2);
    }
    .components .eyebrow {
      margin: 0 0 var(--sp-3);
      color: var(--accent-ink);
      letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    }
    .components a {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 1.5ch;
      white-space: nowrap;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step-0);
      color: var(--text-dim);
      text-decoration: none;
      transition: color 0.15s;
    }
    .components a:hover {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    .components a:focus-visible {
      outline: none;
      box-shadow: var(--ring);
    }
    .components b {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    /* anchor targets stop clear of the 60px sticky header */
    section[id],
    .prose-page[id] {
      scroll-margin-top: 60px;
    }

    /* one column under the global 760px breakpoint: the TOC stacks above the
       article the way a docs site collapses its sidebar */
    @media (max-width: 760px) {
      #doc {
        display: block;
      }
      .components {
        position: static;
        margin-bottom: var(--sp-6);
        max-height: none;
        overflow: visible;
      }
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .hide-sm {
        display: none;
      }
      .hud-identity h1 {
        font-size: var(--step-2);
      }
      .hud-metrics {
        top: auto;
        bottom: var(--sp-7);
        right: var(--sp-4);
      }
      .hud-compass {
        display: none;
      }
      .node-arts {
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: var(--sp-6);
      }
      .node-art {
        width: min(58vw, 300px);
      }
    }

    /* the global reduced-motion rule kills transitions only; freeze the diagrams too */
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .fieldmap *,
      .fieldmap,
      .diagram * {
        animation: none !important;
      }
    }

    /* ---- section diagrams --------------------------------------------------
       Shared chrome for every exhibit diagram: HUD corner brackets on the
       figure, no title, no caption, no border, drawing running to the corners.
       Per-diagram vocabulary follows in its own block. */
    .diagram {
      position: relative;
      margin: var(--sp-5) 0 0;
      max-width: 860px;
    }
    .diagram::before,
    .diagram::after {
      content: '';
      position: absolute;
      width: 12px;
      height: 12px;
      opacity: 0.7;
    }
    .diagram::before {
      top: 0;
      left: 0;
      border-top: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
      border-left: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
    }
    .diagram::after {
      bottom: 0;
      right: 0;
      border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
      border-right: 1.5px solid var(--text-mute);
    }
    /* brackets stay on the figure; only the drawing scrolls on narrow windows */
    .diagram .scroller {
      overflow-x: auto;
    }
    .diagram svg {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      min-width: 600px;
    }
    .diagram .tag.sub {
      font-size: 13px;
      opacity: 0.75;
    }

    /* ---- mesh-radio diagram ------------------------------------------------
       fieldmap vocabulary at article scale: mono tags, dashed range rings, the
       map's glyph defs reused. */
    .meshdiagram .range {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--border-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-dasharray: 2 6;
    }
    .meshdiagram .range.mine {
      stroke: var(--stone);
      stroke-width: 1.3;
    }
    /* both channels dashed: private long-dashed accent, public dotted grey.
       shared with the field map's mesh layer */
    .meshdiagram .priv-link,
    .map .priv-link {
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 6 6;
      opacity: 0.6;
    }
    .meshdiagram .pub-link,
    .map .pub-link {
      stroke: var(--text-mute);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 2 7;
      opacity: 0.6;
    }
    /* background terrain reads as texture, not content */
    .meshdiagram .contours {
      opacity: 0.45;
    }
    .meshdiagram .sun {
      color: var(--text-mute);
      overflow: visible;
    }
    /* endpoint key badges, shown while an exchange is in flight */
    .meshdiagram .key,
    .map .key {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
      opacity: 0;
      overflow: visible;
    }
    .meshdiagram .key.live,
    .map .key.live {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    .meshdiagram .key.pub,
    .map .key.pub {
      color: var(--text-mute);
    }
    /* community radios light, my contacts darker + sun badge, my node darkest */
    .meshdiagram .glyph {
      color: var(--ash);
      transition: color 0.15s;
    }
    .meshdiagram .contact .glyph {
      color: var(--field-grey);
    }
    .meshdiagram .m-mine .glyph {
      color: var(--text);
    }
    /* set by the traffic script while a wave front is crossing the node.
       accent is reserved for my network handling its own private traffic;
       strangers, and my nodes relaying stranger packets, just darken */
    .meshdiagram .glyph.rx {
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .meshdiagram .contact .glyph.rx,
    .meshdiagram .m-mine .glyph.rx {
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .meshdiagram .glyph.rx.pub {
      color: var(--slate-char); /* grey, never accent, even on my network */
    }
    /* keyed up: wave arcs show only while the node is actually transmitting */
    .meshdiagram .glyph.tx {
      --sig: 1;
    }
    /* one-shot wave, map idiom: the script parks a pooled circle at the
       transmitting node and fires it; full RF range in 0.95s */
    .meshdiagram .pulse {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
      transform-box: fill-box;
      transform-origin: center;
      opacity: 0;
    }
    .meshdiagram .pulse.live {
      animation: mesh-ping 0.95s linear;
    }
    /* public packets ring in grey wherever they are, even relayed by my
       network; accent pulses belong to private traffic only */
    .meshdiagram .pulse.pub {
      stroke: var(--text-mute);
    }
    @keyframes mesh-ping {
      0% { transform: scale(0.02); opacity: 0.65; }
      100% { transform: scale(1); opacity: 0; }
    }
    .meshdiagram .packet,
    .map .packet {
      fill: var(--accent);
      opacity: 0;
    }
    .meshdiagram .packet.live,
    .map .packet.live {
      opacity: 1;
    }

    /* ---- satellite-downlink diagram ----------------------------------------
       Chrome, mono tags and the reduced-motion freeze come from .diagram. The
       standing vocabulary is deliberately the mesh diagram's, at 200x the scale:
       terrain backmost as texture, a dashed ring for radio range, the same grey
       receive flash. There is no accent anywhere in this diagram, on purpose: a
       satellite broadcast is a public payload, and color follows the payload. */
    /* darker than the other scene diagrams' terrain, and deliberately so: here
       the contours are the thing the pass produced, not texture behind it */
    .satdiagram .contours {
      opacity: 0.7;
    }
    /* a faint ground tint under the contours, inside the same masked group, so
       freshly scanned ground reads as ground rather than as blank page and the
       leading edge of a sweep is visible even over featureless terrain */
    .satdiagram .scan-wash {
      fill: var(--surface-sunk);
    }
    /* the map's graticule stroke is --border, which is the ground wash's own
       value and would disappear into it here, so this surface takes the next
       step down the ramp and then holds it back with opacity: furniture has to
       survive the wash without ever outweighing the tracks drawn over it */
    .satdiagram .graticule line {
      stroke: var(--border-strong);
      opacity: 0.5;
    }
    .satdiagram .contours path {
      stroke: var(--ash);
    }
    .satdiagram .contours path.index {
      stroke: var(--field-grey);
    }
    /* a finished strip ages for the entire wait until its own orbit returns:
       the three other passes plus the four gaps between them, 3 x 8.46s + 4 x 3s
       = 37.4s. linear, so it is an even continuous decay rather than a fade that
       happens at some point. tied to track length, speed and PASS_GAP_MS in the
       script: change these together. entering-only, so a repaint snaps back to
       full instead of easing up into it. */
    #sat-scan-mask rect[data-swath] {
      transition: none;
    }
    #sat-scan-mask rect[data-swath].stale {
      opacity: 0;
      transition: opacity 37.4s linear;
    }
    .satdiagram .range {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--stone);
      stroke-width: 1.3;
      stroke-dasharray: 2 6;
    }
    /* the node receives an open broadcast, so it darkens within the greys and
       never borrows accent, exactly as a mesh node relaying a stranger's packet */
    .satdiagram .glyph.rx.pub {
      color: var(--slate-char);
    }
    .satdiagram .track {
      stroke: var(--border-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-dasharray: 1 8; /* dotted, so it never reads as a mesh link */
      opacity: 0.7;
    }
    .satdiagram .sat-glyph {
      color: var(--text-dim);
    }
    .satdiagram .node-glyph {
      color: var(--text);
    }
    /* --sig is never set on this surface, so the node's antenna arcs, the mark
       this page uses for transmitting, cannot appear. That absence is the
       receive-only claim. */
    .satdiagram .glyph.off {
      opacity: 0;
    }
    /* one-shot carrier, mesh idiom: parked at the satellite and fired out to the
       footprint radius. 2s pairs with FRONT_MS in the script, change together. */
    .satdiagram .carrier {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--text-mute);
      stroke-width: 1.25;
      vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
      transform-box: fill-box;
      transform-origin: center;
      opacity: 0;
    }
    .satdiagram .carrier.live {
      animation: sat-carrier 2s linear;
    }
    @keyframes sat-carrier {
      0% { transform: scale(0.02); opacity: 0.7; }
      90% { opacity: 0.12; }
      100% { transform: scale(1); opacity: 0; }
    }
    /* the static frame's broadcast, caught partway to the node */
    .satdiagram .carrier-static {
      opacity: 0.55;
    }
    .satdiagram .carrier-static.off {
      opacity: 0;
    }

    .prose-page {
      padding-top: 0; /* #doc carries the top space now */
    }

    /* ---- homestead layer cards (preamble) ----------------------------------
       The four layers a homestead needs, of which this document covers one.
       Rides the global .grid/.panel pair, so it inherits the two-column-to-one
       collapse at 640px rather than defining a breakpoint of its own. */
    .layers {
      margin-top: var(--sp-5);
    }
    .layer h3 {
      margin: 0 0 var(--sp-3);
      font-size: var(--step-0);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }
    /* each row's glyph IS the bullet, so no marker and no list indent */
    .layer ul {
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
      color: var(--text-dim);
    }
    .layer li {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.8ch;
    }
    .layer li + li {
      margin-top: var(--sp-2);
    }
    /* Icon.astro pins its glyph at 1em, so scale it off the row's font size */
    .layer li :global(svg) {
      width: 1.15em;
      height: 1.15em;
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    .prose-page p,
    .marks > p {
      margin: var(--sp-5) 0 0;
      max-width: 62ch;
      color: var(--text-dim);
    }
    /* BLUF: every section opens with its conclusion, so the lead reads at full
       ink and a shorter measure and the supporting prose stays dimmed */
    .prose-page > .lead,
    .marks > .lead {
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: var(--step-1);
      max-width: 54ch;
      border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
      padding-left: var(--sp-4);
    }
    /* the fixed sub-heads inside a section. same treatment as .layer h3, one
       step of space above so a subsection reads as a break rather than a label */
    /* direct children only: the layer cards above carry their own h3 rule and
       must not inherit the section spacing */
    .prose-page > h3,
    .marks > h3 {
      margin: var(--sp-6) 0 0;
      font-size: var(--step-0);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }
    .prose-page > h3 + p,
    .marks > h3 + p {
      margin-top: var(--sp-3);
    }
    /* chapter eyebrows. the nav's are rendered in this template (scoped); the
       body's come through head()'s set:html, so that one needs :global */
    .components .chapter {
      margin: var(--sp-4) 0 0;
      color: var(--text-mute);
    }
    .marks :global(.chapter) {
      margin: 0 0 var(--sp-3);
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    /* The logo rows are hidden, not removed: the markup, the tools.js data and
       the Mark/AltPair components all stay in place, and the row's own layout
       is kept below. Switch display back to flex to bring every chip back. */
    .mark-row {
      display: none;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: center;
      gap: var(--sp-3);
      margin: var(--sp-4) 0 0;
    }
    :global(.mark) {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.6ch;
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: var(--step--1);
      color: var(--text-dim);
      text-decoration: none;
      border: var(--border-w) solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
      padding: 0.4em 0.8em;
      transition: color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s;
    }
    :global(.mark:hover) {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
      border-color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    :global(.mark:focus-visible) {
      outline: none;
      box-shadow: var(--ring);
    }
    :global(.mark svg) {
      width: 1.1em;
      height: 1.1em;
      flex: none;
      color: var(--text-mute);
      transition: color 0.15s;
    }
    :global(.mark:hover svg) {
      color: var(--accent-ink);
    }
    :global(.alt-pair) {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.5ch;
      border: var(--border-w) solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
      padding: 0.4em 0.8em;
    }
    :global(.alt-pair .mark) {
      border: none;
      padding: 0;
    }
    :global(.alt-pair .closed) {
      color: var(--text-mute);
    }
    :global(.alt-arrow) {
      width: 0.9em;
      height: 0.9em;
      color: var(--text-mute);
      flex: none;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
